"exact token usage\" as output – we can report actual used tokens. The phrase \"exact global budget\" might mean the budget is a hard upper bound, not necessarily exactly reached. However the word \"exact\" suggests equality. I'll stick to greedy as described. We must also check feasibility: sum of forced lengths > token_budget -> raise ValueError. Also ensure forced_words list indices are valid.\n\nReturn value: should include selected_word_indices (list of ints in original order? The greedy adds words in order of ranking, but we need to return them in original order for \"faithful_reconstruction\"? The problem says \"Return words and tokens in original order with exact token usage.\" So we need to sort selected word indices by their original order (i.e., ascending index). Also tokens: we need to list the token indices in the order they appear when concatenating selected words in original order. That is faithful reconstruction: the sequence of tokens as they appear in the original order of selected words, preserving the internal order of tokens within each word. So we should flatten the token indices of selected words in the order of original word indices.\n\nThus output: {\"selected_word_indices\": list of ints sorted ascending, \"selected_token_indices\": list of ints in the order they appear, \"total_tokens_used\": int}. Also maybe include \"word_scores_used\"? Not required. \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\" - could include input case_id? But function only receives case dict, can return case_id as well? The problem says \"return a JSON-serializable object\". Usually we just return the reconstruction result. We'll include fields: \"selected_word_indices\", \"selected_token_indices\", \"total_tokens_used\". Also \"forced_words\" are included implicitly.\n\nEdge cases: ties by score -> use source position (i.e., original index) as tie-breaker: lower index first. In ranking, after forced, we sort by score descending, then by original index ascending.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate case dict has required keys. Raise ValueError if missing or invalid types.\n2. Extract word_token_indices, word_scores, forced_words, token_budget.\n3. Check lengths: len(word_token_indices) == len(word_scores). Also forced_words indices inside range.\n4. Compute forced_word_lengths and forced_total = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_words).\n5. If forced_total > token_budget: raise ValueError(\"Forced words exceed budget\")\n6. Initialize selected_set = set(forced_words) (but we need order later)\n7. remaining_budget = token_budget - forced_total\n8. Create list of remaining words: indices not in forced_set, with (score, index, length)\n9. Sort: primary key score descending, secondary key index ascending.\n10. For each word in sorted list:\n    if length <= remaining_budget:\n        add index to selected_set, remaining_budget -= length\n   (Note: after adding, continue looping; we don't skip others? Greedy will add as many as possible in that order. That's fine.)\n11. Build selected_word_indices = sorted(selected_set) (by index).\n12. For each word index in selected_word_indices (ascending), extend token list with word_token_indices[word_index].\n13. total_tokens_used = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in selected_word_indices) (should equal token_budget - remaining_budget)\n14. Return dict with selected_word_indices, selected_token_indices, total_tokens_used.\n\nCheck if remaining_budget could be positive after greedy? If exact budget required, we might need to adjust but per problem description, we accept because it says \"Select forced words first ... Accept a word only when its entire token group fits the residual budget.\" It doesn't say we must exactly fill. So we just output used tokens. The phrase \"exact token usage\" in output means we report exactly how many tokens used. The budget is a constraint: we cannot exceed. So it's okay.\n\nWe must handle empty forced_words: list can be em